  "sealedCause": {
    "sha256": "893565b9aea09ad933cad0508b00a29a9b90c1d698d2ab90229aa73a6a9f6db2",
    "establishedBy": "operator experiment outside the trial, before the runs",
    "summary": "Playwright 1.58.2 does force-kill the browser 30 s after a close request (closeOrKill in lib/server/browserType.js). It then waits for the child close event, which needs the child standard streams to close. Chrome has by then started its updater and a crash handler in other process groups; they survive the kill and hold the inherited streams open until they exit."
  },
  "finding": "The Fawn arm absorbed the source change: on resume the workspace re-read the changed files, the one conclusion resting on them was marked for recheck and was rewritten against the new code, and the final records say the CDP adapter is now bounded. Its diagnosis of the Playwright side is wrong: it supports no deadline and rejects the 30 s deadline that the source implements, because the deciding code sits at character 11126 of a 15435-character file and saved evidence is shown 6000 characters at a time. No comparison with stock automation is established.",
  "limits": [
    "One pair, and the control arm of the valid run was cut short by a harness budget cap; nothing here compares Fawn with stock automation.",
